{% extends "@core/docs/_layout.twig" %}

{% block doc_content %}
    <h1>The Request Object</h1>
    <p>
        The <code>Rhapsody\Core\Request</code> class provides an object-oriented way to interact with the current HTTP request. It encapsulates all the information about the request, such as GET and POST data, server variables, and uploaded files, making your application more secure and easier to work with.
    </p>
    <p>
        An instance of the <code>Request</code> object is automatically created and passed as the first argument to every controller method by the router.
    </p>

    <hr>

    <h2>Accessing Request Information</h2>

    <h3>Route Pattern Matching</h3>
    <p>
        The <code>is()</code> method allows you to check if the request's path matches a given pattern.
        This is particularly useful for middleware to conditionally apply logic based on the route.
    </p>
    <p>
        The method supports a trailing wildcard (<code>*</code>) to match any subpath:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">// Check if the current request is for the API
    if ($request->is('api/*')) {
        // API-specific handling
    }

    // Check for exact match
    if ($request->is('users')) {
        // Users page
    }

    // Check for admin routes
    if ($request->is('admin/*')) {
        // Admin routes
    }</code></pre>
    <p>
        The <code>is()</code> method normalizes both the request path and the pattern by stripping
        leading slashes, making it consistent for matching.
    </p>

    <h3>Get the Request Path &amp; Method</h3>
    <pre><code class="language-php">// In a controller method
public function updateUser(Request $request, string $id): Response
{
    // Get the request method (e.g., 'get', 'post')
    $method = $request->getMethod(); // "post"

    // Get the clean URI path (e.g., '/users/1')
    $path = $request->getPath(); // "/users/1"
    
    // ...
}</code></pre>

    <h3>Retrieving Input (GET, POST, JSON, PUT, etc.)</h3>
    <p>
        The <code>getBody()</code> method intelligently handles all input sources:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>If the <code>Content-Type</code> is <code>application/json</code>, it decodes the JSON payload.</li>
        <li>For standard form submissions (<code>application/x-www-form-urlencoded</code>), it returns <code>$_POST</code>.</li>
        <li>For <code>PUT</code>, <code>PATCH</code>, or <code>DELETE</code> requests with form data, it parses <code>php://input</code> automatically.</li>
    </ul>
    <pre><code class="language-php">$allInput = $request->getBody();
$email    = $allInput['email'] ?? null;</code></pre>

    <h3>Typed Input Accessors</h3>
    <p>
        <code>string()</code>, <code>int()</code>, <code>bool()</code> and <code>array()</code> read a single input value and hand it back as exactly the type you asked for, so you don't need casts or <code>isset()</code> checks. They look in the request body first (form fields or a JSON payload, as described above) and then fall back to the query string.
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">$name   = $request->string('name');        // '' when missing
$page   = $request->int('page', 1);        // 1 when missing or not an integer
$active = $request->bool('active');        // false when missing
$tags   = $request->array('tags');         // [] when missing or not an array
                                           // ?tags[]=php&amp;tags[]=twig  gives  ['php', 'twig']</code></pre>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-full text-left border border-gray-300 dark:border-gray-700">
            <thead class="bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800">
                <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-300 dark:border-gray-700">Method</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-300 dark:border-gray-700">Accepts</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-300 dark:border-gray-700">Gives the default when</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>string($key, $default = '')</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">Strings, ints, floats and bools (converted to a string)</td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">The value is missing, <code>null</code>, or an array</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>int($key, $default = 0)</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">Ints and integer-like strings such as <code>'42'</code> or <code>'-7'</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">The value is anything else, including <code>'1.5'</code>, <code>'12abc'</code>, an empty string, or a decimal number</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>bool($key, $default = false)</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>true</code>/<code>false</code>, <code>1</code>/<code>0</code>, and the strings <code>true</code>, <code>false</code>, <code>on</code>, <code>off</code>, <code>yes</code>, <code>no</code> (case-insensitive)</td><td class="px-4 py-2 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">The value is anything else. An empty string is read as <code>false</code>, not as the default</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-4 py-2"><code>array($key)</code></td><td class="px-4 py-2">Arrays, such as <code>tags[]=a&amp;tags[]=b</code> or a JSON array</td><td class="px-4 py-2">The value is missing or not an array (it always returns <code>[]</code>; there is no default argument)</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <p>A typical controller reads like this:</p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">public function showUsers(Request $request): Response
{
    $page       = $request->int('page', 1);
    $sort       = $request->string('sort', 'name');
    $subscribed = $request->bool('subscribed');   // an unchecked checkbox is never submitted, so this is false
    $roles      = $request->array('roles');

    // ...
}</code></pre>

    <h3>What the Accessors Do Not Do</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>They never throw.</strong> A bad or missing value quietly becomes the default. If a value is required, check it with the <a href="{{ base_url }}/docs/validation">Validator</a> and reject the request. (This is the opposite of the <code>Config</code> reader, which throws on a missing or wrongly-typed key. See <a href="{{ base_url }}/docs/support">Support Helpers</a>.)</li>
        <li><strong>They don't validate or sanitize.</strong> <code>int()</code> guarantees you receive an integer, not a sensible one, so clamp or range-check values like page numbers and limits yourself. <code>string()</code> returns the text exactly as it was sent, without the HTML-encoding that <code>getQueryParam()</code> applies, so escape it on output (Twig does this automatically) and never build SQL from it.</li>
        <li><strong>They work on the Request's own data.</strong> Unlike <code>getQueryParam()</code>, which reads PHP's <code>$_GET</code> directly, these methods use the data the <code>Request</code> was built with. That makes them work with requests created by <code>Request::create()</code> in your tests (see <a href="{{ base_url }}/docs/testing">Unit Testing</a>).</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>input() and all()</h3>
    <p>
        The accessors above are built on <code>input()</code>, which returns a single raw value (body first, then the query string) or a default of your choosing. <code>all()</code> returns every input value at once: the query string and the request body merged, with body values winning when a key appears in both.
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">$value = $request->input('q', 'none');   // raw value, any type
$data  = $request->all();                // query string + body</code></pre>

    <h3>Retrieving Individual Query Parameters</h3>
    <p>To get a value from the URL's query string (e.g., <code>/users?page=2</code>), use the <code>getQueryParam()</code> method. It includes a second argument to specify a default value if the parameter is not present.</p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">public function showUsers(Request $request): Response
{
    $currentPage = (int) $request->getQueryParam('page', 1);
    // ...
}</code></pre>

    <h3>All Query Parameters</h3>
    <p>
        Use <code>allQueryParams()</code> to retrieve an associative array of <strong>all</strong> GET parameters from the current request. This is useful when you need to inspect or forward the entire query string.
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">$params = $request->allQueryParams();
// e.g., ['page' => 2, 'sort' => 'asc', 'filter' => 'active']</code></pre>

    <h3>Canonical URL</h3>
    <p>
        The <code>getCanonicalUrl()</code> method builds a clean, SEO‑friendly canonical URL for the current request. It automatically:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>Detects the correct protocol (<code>http://</code> or <code>https://</code>).</li>
        <li>Uses the host from <code>$_SERVER['HTTP_HOST']</code>.</li>
        <li>Strips the path to its bare structure (no extra text).</li>
        <li>Retains only allowed query parameters (by default, only <code>page</code> is kept).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        This method is used internally by the main layout to generate the <code>&lt;link rel="canonical"&gt;</code> tag, but you can also call it manually if you need the value elsewhere.
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">// Keep only 'page' and 'category' in the canonical URL
$canonical = $request->getCanonicalUrl(['page', 'category']);</code></pre>

    <h3>Retrieving Uploaded Files</h3>
    <p>You can access information about uploaded files using the <code>getFiles()</code> method, which returns the contents of the <code>$_FILES</code> superglobal.</p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">public function handleUpload(Request $request): Response
{
    $files = $request->getFiles();
    $avatar = $files['avatar'] ?? null;
    
    if ($avatar && $avatar['error'] === UPLOAD_ERR_OK) {
        // Process the uploaded file
    }
    
    // ...
}</code></pre>

    <h3>Direct Access to Raw Data</h3>
    <p>If you need to access raw GET or POST values directly, you can use the <code>get()</code> and <code>post()</code> methods:</p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">$userId = $request->get('user_id');   // from $_GET
$email  = $request->post('email');    // from $_POST</code></pre>

{% endblock %}